@page "/docs/tools/vs-code-add"

@* The extension's catalog and everything that adds to an app (0.9.4: out/catalog.js, components.js, dragDrop.js,
   itemPage.js, form.js, formPanel.js, extension.js newPage/newComponent). Each command is one of izzy's: this page
   says where it is in the editor and links the command's own page. New Page's "Start from" is izzy add
   page --from (built Nov 26, 2026). *@

<ArticlePage Section="tools" Slug="vs-code-add">
    <Lead>
        Browse every component, block and template in the side bar and read its reference in the editor.
        Insert a component or a block at the cursor, add a page, or create an app from a template.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Browse</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Components lists every component by category, with the ones you used lately under Recent. Type in
            the view, or use its search button, to filter the list. Catalog lists the starters, app kits, pages
            and blocks, and labels the ones that need Pro. Search Catalog finds any item by
            name, components too.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Select an item to open its reference in an editor tab. A component's has every option with its type
            and default, its events, slots and parts, and its usage examples. A block's has its picture, its
            render modes and its main settings. A starter's or kit's also has its screens at desktop and phone
            sizes. The pictures load from izzyblazor.com.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Create an app</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            New App starts from a starter or an app kit. Give the app a name and a folder, fill in its settings,
            and confirm the list of files. Set Up IzzyBlazor in This App adds IzzyBlazor to a Blazor app you
            have. See <Anchor Href="docs/tools/templates">Templates</Anchor> and
            <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/existing-app">Add to an existing app</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Insert at the cursor</Heading>
        <Table Items="Ways" Caption="Ways to put a component or block in a page" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-prose" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Use" Width="Length.Rem(12)">@context!.Name</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What it does">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            A component goes in as its tag, with no review: undo takes it back. When the cursor is inside another tag's attributes, the new tag goes
            after that tag, so the page still builds. Markup can't go inside <Code>@@code</Code>: move the cursor
            into the markup first. A block with settings asks for them, then shows the changes to review. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/tools/components">Components</Anchor> and
            <Anchor Href="docs/tools/blocks">Blocks</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Add pages and components</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The first four commands are in the Project view, under Add. New Page and New Component are also in
            the IzzyBlazor menu of a right-click on a folder or in a <Code>.razor</Code> file.
        </TextBlock>
        <Table Items="Adds" Caption="Commands that add a file to the app" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-prose" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Command" Width="Length.Rem(12)">@context!.Name</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What it makes">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Read"><Anchor Href="@context!.Href">@context.Link</Anchor></TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            New Page's Start from makes a list, a detail page or a form with sample data, with a free account.
            For a page that runs in the browser (Interactive WebAssembly or Auto), start New Page from a
            folder of the app's <Code>.Client</Code> project.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Settings forms</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A catalog item with settings asks for them first: as steps at the top of the window, or as a form
            that shows what the item would create and change as you fill it in.
            <Anchor Href="docs/tools/vs-code#settings"><Code>izzyblazor.formStyle</Code></Anchor> says which.
            A choice that needs Pro is labeled Pro, and says so when you pick it.
        </TextBlock>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record Row(string Name, string Text);

    private sealed record Add(string Name, string Text, string Href, string Link);

    private static readonly Row[] Ways =
    [
        new("The + button", "On a component or block in the side bar or on its reference: inserts it in the .razor file you were last in, at the cursor. The reference names that file and line."),
        new("Ctrl+Alt+I", "Opens a search at the cursor for any component or block, recent components first. Cmd+Option+I on a Mac."),
        new("Drag", "Drag a component or block from the side bar into the page, and hold Shift as you drop. Without Shift, VS Code opens what you dragged. After you drop a component, VS Code's Drop with button switches between its tag and its usage example."),
        new("Insert Example", "Inserts one of a component's usage examples from the docs, with its @using lines and @code members."),
    ];

    private static readonly Add[] Adds =
    [
        new("New Page", "A .razor page with its route, render mode and layout, and a link in the app's nav unless you clear the box.", "docs/tools/pages", "Pages and components"),
        new("New Component", "A component with no route, in the folder you right-clicked or the open file's folder.", "docs/tools/pages", "Pages and components"),
        new("Page for a Model", "A page that lists, adds, edits and deletes a model class's records. Also in a right-click on a .cs file. It finds the classes through the C# extension.", "docs/tools/crud", "CRUD pages"),
        new("Block as a Page", "A block from the catalog as a page of its own.", "docs/tools/blocks", "Blocks"),
        new("Add to App", "On a block's reference or its right-click in Catalog: adds it as a file of its own, when you have no .razor file open to insert it in.", "docs/tools/blocks", "Blocks"),
    ];
}
